  {
    "episode_id": 72784,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72784.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 1,
    "turn_text": "When it came to the floor, Chairman Roberts made numerous attempts to try and reach a compromise with her. She rejected that compromise. So he went back to the drawing board, made further adjustments, trying to build on a provision that would trigger a mandatory labeling process, but that was rejected as well. So it just seems like the Senate Democrats are having a hard time saying yes to anything. And by refusing to compromise on the bill, Senate Democrats appear to be willing to allow food costs for American families to increase by an average of about $1,000 a year, because that's what this increased labeling cost is going to do. So I strongly supported a voluntary only GMO food label, but I believe what happened on the floor, the attempts that Senator Roberts made, the concessions that were made to try and get a bipartisan bill was something I could support. But I'll tell you right now, I just think the terms of next steps, the balls in the Democrats' court, there were lots of attempts made to try and find some common ground and they were turned back at every opportunity by the Democrats. So we'll see when Senator comes back from the Easter recess if we get to think back on track.",
