    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72784,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72784.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, in Washington, D.C., that's a code for, I'll work with you if you get me my way. And she seems to be responding to a very vocal group, minority group of people out there, and they don't represent the silent majority on this issue. An issue on which the science is very clear. Foods made with GMO ingredients have been proven safe by sound scientific research. We know we've got to feed an additional 2 billion people on the planet by the year 2050 and we're not going to be able to do that without genetically modified crops. In fact, if you look at the recent studies of food processors, companies, if they had to label their products in accordance with the Vermont law, which is scheduled, as I said, to go into effect on July 1, The added additional cost to consumers nationwide is going to be $87 billion. I mean, that's $1,400 per South Dakota family. So that's what an immediate mandatory label would do. And that's why we were trying so hard to get an agreement with the Democrats and the Senate. But what Senator Staff now is saying is essentially, basically, yeah, give us our way. It will go along with it. We, as I said, reported to be a lot of the Ag Committee that was a voluntary approach, got to the floor, converted it over basically to a mandatory approach, phased in over a three-year period, and they still turned it down. So it just seems to me that they just don't want to accept yes for an answer. And for that reason, I think they're going to have to They're going to have to own this issue and all the additional costs that are going to come with it if they are willing to come back to the table.",
