    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72784.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 17,
    "turn_text": "Well, I've been encouraging to see that everybody kind of come together on the budget. You know, it's always been crop insurance in particular, has always been on the chopping block by the Obama administration. In fact, the President's proposal, again, this year, makes draconian cuts to crop insurance, which is the most important safety net program that farmers and ranchers today. And what that shows, I think, is that he's turned a deaf ear to the needs of farmers and ranchers. In fact, the latest proposal would shave $18 billion from the program, and if it's enacted, would make it far-cost layer for South Dakota producers to ensure crops at a time when commodity prices are at their lowest levels in years. So these administration crop insurance proposals would be detrimental to South Dakota Ag producers as well as farmers in our states. And it was encouraging to see that there was strong bipartisan opposition to these cuts across the country, across commodity groups, and that the Those in the kind of in the up and down the food chain came together to make it clear that this is something that shouldn't happen. The farm bill shouldn't be reopened. I mean, that's we pass a farm bill. It audit that people ought to be able to know those are going to be the rules of the foreseeable future.",
