    "turn_text": "one year And then, you know, using the other year to do the oversight that we should be doing to make sure these programs are working in the way they were intended would be a very useful model. And if you had to do something in the off year, if you had an emergency or a crisis, you could always do a supplemental bill or something like that. But I just believe that we could benefit enormously as a nation by making some important reforms including going to buy a new budget, but also making the budget process binding. Right now, it's a mining resolution. It gets your team waived. I would do away with baseline budgeting, which builds in automatic increases and spending every year. And that's what keeps the trajectory of federal spending on an upward spike. If we determined year in and year out what was working went to kind of a zero based budgeting approach, I think we would get a much better result. So there are a number of forms that need to be made, and I hope that this year we get serious about doing that. I've been carrying that message in our meetings with my colleagues in the Senate, and it seems like we're making some headway. A lot of the freshmen class and new people who have come in and share that same idea and goal because they've seen how it works. and uh... realize that this is not uh... this is a broken process",
